.TH STYLEFIX 1 "October 2026" "stylefix 0.8.2" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
stylefix \- regularize a Markdown file to US or UK English and a style guide
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B stylefix
.I input.md
.B \-\-target
.RB { us | uk }
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B stylefix
takes a Markdown file and writes a copy whose spelling, punctuation and
dates follow one English variety (US or UK) and one style guide (Chicago
Manual of Style or Oxford / New Hart's Rules). It is fully automatic and
runs locally unless pointed at a hosted model: Vale decides what to change,
a small wrapper applies the changes, and a language model settles the few
words that depend on meaning. Fenced code, inline code, link targets, URLs and YAML front matter
are never modified.
.PP
Every Vale alert lands in one of three tiers:
.TP
.B deterministic
The productive endings (-ise/-ize, -yse/-yze, -our/-or, -re/-er, -ll-/-l-)
are handled as patterns: a hit is rewritten and confirmed against the
shipped en_US and en_GB word lists, so coinages convert without being
listed and words already valid in the target (advertise, precise, glamour)
are left alone. Everything else comes from VarCon, the SCOWL project's
table of American, British and Oxford spellings. Style preferences
(towards/toward) are table swaps. Replacements preserve the original
word's case.
.TP
.B adjudicated
Words whose correct form depends on sense or part of speech
(licence/license, practice/practise, programme/program, cheque/check,
storey/story, tyre/tire, metre/meter and about 150 more, identified from
VarCon's sense annotations and glossed with its notes), pattern hits whose rewrite is in no dictionary (a coinage,
a name or a typo), and any replacement that sits inside a quotation or is
capitalised mid-sentence (a possible proper name or title), are sent to
the model as a multiple-choice question with the
surrounding sentence. The model picks one of the supplied options or KEEP;
it never rewrites prose. Invalid or low-confidence answers are not applied.
.TP
.B review
Everything else is listed in the report and left alone: words that carry
the other variety's ending, are in its dictionary and not the target's, yet
could not be converted; and, when no model is configured, the serial-comma
and numeral hits.
.PP
After the word-level edits a punctuation pass converts straight quotes to
curly, sets the primary quotation style (double for US, single for UK) with
correct nesting, fixes apostrophes, normalizes parenthetical dashes
(unspaced em dash for Chicago, spaced en dash for Oxford), puts en dashes in
number ranges, and reorders dates (September 4, 2026 for US, 4 September
2026 for UK). Chicago also moves commas and periods inside closing quotes.
The UK direction never moves punctuation outside quotes, because whether it
belongs to the quotation is not knowable mechanically.
.SH STYLE GUIDES
.TP
.B chicago
Unspaced em dash, serial comma required, numbers below 101 spelled out,
toward/among/while, commas and periods inside closing quotes. US, UK, PhD
without periods; 10 a.m.; vs.; e.g.
.TP
.B gpo
As chicago but numerals from 10, and U.S., U.K., Ph.D. with periods. The
federal standard.
.TP
.B ap
Spaced em dash, serial comma omitted in a simple series, numerals from 10,
toward, punctuation inside quotes. U.S., U.K., Ph.D. with periods.
.TP
.B apa
Unspaced em dash, serial comma required, numerals from 10, punctuation
inside quotes. U.S. with periods; PhD without.
.TP
.B oxford
Spaced en dash, Oxford comma required, numbers below 101 spelled out,
towards/amongst, logical punctuation (placement left as written). US, UK,
PhD without points; vs without a point.
.TP
.B guardian
Spaced en dash, serial comma omitted unless needed, numerals from 10,
towards, logical punctuation. US, UK, PhD; 10am; v; eg, ie, etc without
points.
.TP
.B none
No guide. Spelling and the target variety's punctuation still apply (curly
quotes in the variety's primary style, apostrophes, en dashes in ranges,
date order). Parenthetical dashes, serial commas, numerals, toward/towards
and punctuation placement are left exactly as written.
.PP
Abbreviations follow the guides' general rules. Chicago, Oxford and the
Guardian: no periods in any abbreviation in capitals (US, EU, NATO, PhD,
DC), applied to every period-group of capitals; a group followed by a
capitalised word may end its sentence, continue a name, or be a person's
initials, and is adjudicated. AP and GPO: periods in two-letter country,
place, degree and era abbreviations (U.S., U.K., D.C., B.A., Ph.D., B.C.),
none in EU or in longer ones. APA: U.S. with periods, degrees without.
Clock times, vs., e.g., i.e. and etc. take each guide's form.
.PP
GPO and APA use U.S. only as an adjective and write the noun as "the
United States". By default under those guides each likely noun use is
listed for review;
.B \-\-abbreviation\-nouns enforce
has the model decide each "the U.S." (noun: United States; adjective: keep
the abbreviation).
.PP
Dashes, toward/towards and punctuation placement are applied outright.
Serial-comma and numeral hits are adjudicated: the model chooses between one
concrete edit and KEEP from the sentence and the guide's rule; without a
model they are listed for review.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Target
.TP
.BR \-\-target " {us|uk}"
English variety to convert to. Required.
.TP
.BR \-\-style " GUIDE"
Mechanical conventions: dashes, serial commas, numerals, toward/towards,
punctuation placement. One of chicago (Chicago Manual of Style; US
default), gpo (US Government Publishing Office Style Manual), ap
(Associated Press), apa (APA 7th), oxford (New Hart's Rules; UK default),
guardian (Guardian/Observer), none. Spelling always follows \-\-target.
.TP
.BR \-\-abbreviation\-nouns " {default|warn|enforce|off}"
The GPO/APA rule that U.S. is an adjective only. default: warn when the
guide has the rule.
.TP
.B \-\-list\-styles
Describe every style guide and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-oxford\-ize
With
.BR "\-\-target uk" ,
keep \-ize spellings (Oxford) instead of converting them to \-ise.
.TP
.BI \-\-accept " FILE"
Extra words or phrases to leave alone, one per line, # comments allowed.
Added to the built-in list of technical and policy terms,
.IR ~/.config/stylefix/accept.txt
and
.IR ./.stylefix/accept.txt
when those exist. Installed as a Vale vocabulary, so every rule skips them.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-default\-accept
Use only the \-\-accept file; ignore the built-in, user and project lists.
.SS Output
.TP
.BI \-o ", " \-\-output " FILE"
Where to write the result. Default:
.IR input . target .md
(for example draft.us.md).
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-in\-place
Overwrite the input file.
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
Print a unified diff to standard output and write nothing.
.TP
.BI \-\-report " FILE"
Where to write the report. Default:
.IR output .report.md.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-report
Do not write a report.
.TP
.B \-\-changes
Print every change to standard error as it is made: line number, the word
before and after, the surrounding text, and how it was decided (auto, or
the model's confidence and reason). Words the model chose to keep and
words left undecided follow, then punctuation counts, then every alert
left for human review, grouped by check.
.BR \-v
implies this.
.SS Model
.TP
.BR \-\-llm " {auto|ollama|none}"
Endpoint choice.
.B auto
(the default) uses the endpoint configured by flags, environment or
config file (see
.BR ENVIRONMENT " and " FILES ),
or else a local Ollama with a model pulled, or else no model.
.B ollama
uses the local Ollama server and its first installed model, ignoring
configured endpoints.
.B none
skips the model: ambiguous words are left as they are and listed in the
report, while capitalised deterministic replacements are applied anyway.
.TP
.BI \-\-url " URL"
Any OpenAI-compatible base URL ending in /v1. Overrides every configured url.
A \-\-url or STYLEFIX_LLM_URL drops keys set in lower layers; set
STYLEFIX_LLM_KEY (or \fBapi_key_env\fR in the same config file) alongside it.
.TP
.BI \-\-model " NAME"
Model name at the endpoint. If no layer sets one, the server's first listed model.
.TP
.B \-\-think
Let a reasoning model think before answering. Slower by a factor of four to
ten and slightly more accurate; pair with
.B \-\-batch\-size 8
and
.BR "\-\-timeout 600" .
.TP
.BI \-\-min\-confidence " X"
Decisions reported below this confidence are not applied. Default 0.7.
.TP
.BI \-\-batch\-size " N"
Items per model request. Default 20.
.TP
.BI \-\-parallel " N"
Concurrent model requests. Default 4. A serving stack with continuous
batching (vLLM) scales almost linearly with this.
.TP
.BI \-\-timeout " SEC"
Seconds to wait for one model request, a positive number. Default 120, or
\fBtimeout\fR in config.toml.
.TP
.BI \-\-cache " FILE"
Where model decisions are cached, keyed on target, word and sentence.
Default
.IR .stylefix/cache.json
in the working directory. Editing a cached choice overrides it on the next
run.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-cache
Neither read nor write the cache.
.SS Configuration
.TP
.B \-\-setup
Ask where the model is (local Ollama, a hosted OpenAI-compatible API, or a
self-hosted server), test it, and save the answer as the
.B [llm]
table of
.I ~/.config/adjudicate/config.toml
(shared with proofix) or
.IR ~/.config/stylefix/config.toml .
An existing table is shown and replaced only on confirmation, after a
backup to
.IR config.toml.bak ;
other tables are kept. For a self-hosted server it detects whether to set
.BR thinking_switch .
Needs a terminal. Ends by running the check below.
.TP
.B \-\-check\-endpoint
Print the endpoint stylefix would use, one line per setting with the
layer it came from (a key is never shown), then list the server's models
and send one small request. Honours
.BR \-\-llm ", " \-\-url ", " \-\-model " and " \-\-timeout .
.SS General
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Report model batches on standard error as they are sent.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print the usage summary and exit.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.BR STYLEFIX_LLM_URL ", " STYLEFIX_LLM_MODEL ", " STYLEFIX_LLM_KEY
Endpoint, model and API key for stylefix; below the flags, above the
config files.
.TP
.BR ADJUDICATE_LLM_URL ", " ADJUDICATE_LLM_MODEL ", " ADJUDICATE_LLM_KEY
The same, shared with proofix; below
.I ~/.config/stylefix/config.toml
and above
.IR ~/.config/adjudicate/config.toml .
.TP
.B OLLAMA_HOST
Ollama base URL. Default http://localhost:11434.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/stylefix/config.toml
.TQ
.I ~/.config/adjudicate/config.toml
Table
.B [llm]
with keys
.BR url ", " model ", " api_key " or " api_key_env ", " timeout ", " thinking_switch .
The first applies to stylefix only; the second is shared with proofix. A
file that sets
.B url
discards the model, key and thinking_switch of the layers below it.
.TP
.I ~/.config/stylefix/accept.txt
Personal accept list, one term per line. Honours XDG_CONFIG_HOME.
.TP
.I .stylefix/accept.txt
Project accept list, read from the working directory.
.TP
.IR output .report.md
Counts, every deterministic replacement, every model decision with its
confidence, reason and context, and every alert left for a human, grouped
by Vale check.
.TP
.I .stylefix/cache.json
Model decision cache. Safe to delete; safe to commit if the team wants
reproducible runs.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Finished. Undecided words and review items are reported, not fatal. For
.BR \-\-check\-endpoint " and " \-\-setup :
the endpoint answers.
.TP
.B 1
.BR \-\-check\-endpoint " or " \-\-setup :
no endpoint, or it does not answer; or setup was cancelled or declined.
.TP
.B 2
Input file missing, Vale not found or failed, or an invalid endpoint
configuration (the message names the file and key). For
.BR \-\-setup :
standard input is not a terminal.
.SH EXAMPLES
Write draft.us.md and draft.us.md.report.md using Chicago conventions:
.PP
.nf
    stylefix draft.md \-\-target us
.fi
.PP
British spelling with Oxford \-ize, to a named file:
.PP
.nf
    stylefix draft.md \-\-target uk \-\-oxford\-ize \-o final.md
.fi
.PP
See what would change without writing anything:
.PP
.nf
    stylefix draft.md \-\-target us \-\-dry\-run
.fi
.PP
Careful pass for an important document, protecting house terms:
.PP
.nf
    stylefix draft.md \-\-target us \-\-think \-\-batch\-size 8 \-\-timeout 600 \\
             \-\-accept house\-terms.txt
.fi
.PP
A Word document, via pandoc:
.PP
.nf
    pandoc in.docx \-t gfm \-o in.md
    stylefix in.md \-\-target uk \-o out.md
    pandoc out.md \-o out.docx
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR vale (1),
.BR pandoc (1).
.PP
Source and README: https://github.com/AndrewBroz/stylefix. Word pairs live in
.IR stylefix/wordlist.py ,
ambiguous-word rules in
.IR stylefix/candidates.py ,
style rules as Vale YAML under
.IR stylefix/styles/ .
